  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Express $\\frac{3x+2}{(x-7)^{2}}$ as a sum of partial fractions.\n\n|  | $\\frac{3x+2}{(x-7)^{2}}\\,=\\,\\frac{3}{(x-7)}\\,\\,+$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | $(x-7)^{2}$ |",
  "worked_solution": "Our task is to find $B$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x+2}{(x-7)^{2}}=\\frac{3}{(x-7)}+\\frac{B}{(x-7)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $(x-7)^{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+2 & =3(x-7)+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=7$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(7)+2 & =3(7-7)+B \\\\ 21+2 & =0+B \\\\ B & =23.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{3x+2}{(x-7)^{2}}=\\frac{3}{(x-7)}+\\frac{23}{(x-7)^{2}}.\n$$",
